      "message": "@bsalomon@google.com - what do you think of having reserve() allocate with growth vs reserve_back() to reserve with tight bounds?",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b4c6428_5dddba4e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T15:20:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems like one would expect that if you did:\n\n```\nSkTArray\u003cT\u003e myArray;\nmyArray.reserve(count);\n// populate count items\n```\n\nthat you\u0027d have a tight array.\n\nGiven that only SkPathRef is using it, what if we just renamed it to something like `reserveAtLeast`?",
      "parentUuid": "ff0c691c_5588f5a1",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c86a3c2a_17e989a5",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T15:26:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s interesting that you think it is tight. std::vector is not tight, and neither is SkTDArray. SkTArray seems like the odd one out here.",
      "parentUuid": "4b4c6428_5dddba4e",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f1a9d070_5e397ce1",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T15:50:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Derp, now that you say that, of course std::vector isn\u0027t tight! \n\nShould we only have `_back` methods, one loose and one tight? I.e. `reserve_back()` and `reserve_back_tight()` (or `reserve_back_minimal()`) and no `reserve()`?",
      "parentUuid": "c86a3c2a_17e989a5",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "37cb3572_d53af8d4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T16:04:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Our current course of actions is to unify the container naming to std::vector type names. Having reserve() with loose bounds follows that plan. The current code expects reserve_back() to be tight, but actually doesn\u0027t use reserve() all that much. So, I think we are free to update its behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "f1a9d070_5e397ce1",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "db7054bf_ccbb4440",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T16:13:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, so should the new callsites use `\u003carray\u003e.reserve(\u003carray\u003e.count() + \u003cadditional\u003e)`?\n\nI still seems odd to have such similarly named methods use different policies. What if we renamed `reserve_back` to something different like `extend_capacity`?",
      "parentUuid": "37cb3572_d53af8d4",
      "revId": "a3c9835d94330e11e29af62e7c48b14136b7f965",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
